#{[17592186495046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495046 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186495143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495046 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating a Total Price from a Unit Price" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495046 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186495154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495046 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d550bb4f-72a6-5b74-b050-8fdc2af6b23f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495064 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495064 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495064 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495064 #edb/ref 1071 "Martha wonders what bag of oranges to buy from three different types available at the supermarket:\n\n- Bag $A$ weighs $10$ pounds, and costs $$13.30\\,.$\n\n- Bag $B$ weighs $8$ pounds, and costs $$11.20\\,.$\n\n- Bag $C$ weighs $6$ pounds, and costs $$9.00\\,.$\n\nWhich bag of oranges is the best deal?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495064 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1900fcae-3d76-5147-a463-152f960f7a6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495064 #edb/ref 1074 "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three bags. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of pounds.  \n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of pounds in bag $A$,  we get\n\n$$\n13.30÷10=1.33\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $A$ is $$1.33$  per pound.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of pounds in bag $B$,  we get\n\n$$\n11.20÷8=1.40\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $B$ is $$1.40$  per pound.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of pounds in bag $C$,  we get\n\n$$\n9.00÷6=1.50\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $C$ is $$1.50$  per pound.\n\nFrom the above, we see that bag $A$ has the lowest unit price. Therefore, bag $A$ is the best deal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355713" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495066 #edb/ref 1071 "At the supply store, the unit price of rope is $$10$ per meter. How many meters of rope can Maya buy for $$1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1dc8a6fb-a221-5a8e-ae1b-b509f998884f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495066 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the unit rate of $$10$ per meter (dollars per meter). To find how many meters of rope she can buy for  $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in meters per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal: \n\n$$\n10\\,\\text{dollars per meter}\\,\\to \\,1÷10=0.1\\,\\text{meters per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $0.1$ meters per dollar. Therefore, Maya can buy $0.1$ meters of rope for $1$ dollar." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495071 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495071 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495071 #edb/ref 1071 "Jenny is going to the craft store to buy some supplies. How much would it cost her to buy $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a yard of gold ribbon?\n\n| Item | Price |\n| --- | --- |\n| Gold Ribbon | $$5/\\text{yd}$ |\n| Silver Ribbon | $$4.5/\\text{yd}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a794af8-7414-51a7-a6c7-994b60009b14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495071 #edb/ref 1074 "The unit price of gold ribbon is $$5/\\text{yd}.$ To find the cost of  $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a yard of gold ribbon, we multiply: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\times \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{5}{1}\\times \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 1}{1\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =5÷2 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it would cost Jenny $$2.50$ to buy $\\frac{1}{2}$ of a yard of gold ribbon." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495072 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495072 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495072 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495072 #edb/ref 1071 "Tom wants to buy some oranges. The price of oranges in a nearby store is $$0.75/lb.$ How much would Tom pay for $3$ pounds of oranges?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495072 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c8fda57-c0ae-5471-9e00-bdb61e2342a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495072 #edb/ref 1074 "The unit price of oranges is $$0.75/lb.$ To find the cost of  $3$ pounds of oranges, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.75\\times 3 & =2.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Tom would pay  $$2.25$ for $3$ pounds of oranges." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495075 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4542" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495075 #edb/ref 1071 "Andy sells homemade lemonade for $\\[math]  4$ per liter. Beth wants to buy some lemonade from Andy, but she only has $\\[/math]  1.$ How much lemonade can Beth buy?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495075 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a104615-4887-59c8-9722-bb07b6cca5ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495075 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the unit rate of $\\[math]  4$ per liter (dollars per liter). To find the amount of lemonade that Beth can buy for $\\[/math]  1,$ we need to find the unit rate in liters per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n4 \\,\\textrm{dollars per liter} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{4}  \\,\\textrm{liters per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ liters per dollar. Therefore, Beth can buy $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a liter of lemonade." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495086 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495086 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68939" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495086 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495086 #edb/ref 1071 "Sarah, a registered nurse, charges $$1.25$ per minute for her work in a rehabilitation center. How long should she work for $1$ dollar?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495086 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "517daee3-2d5a-5b03-be17-46f0dc74f72d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495086 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the unit rate of $$1.25$ per minute (dollars per minute). To find how many minutes Sarah should work  for  $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in minutes per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal: \n\n$$\n1.25\\,\\text{dollars per minute}\\,\\to \\,1÷1.25=0.8\\,\\text{minutes per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $0.8$ minutes per dollar. Therefore, Sarah should work  $0.8$ minutes for $1$ dollar." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495092 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495092 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355005" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495092 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495092 #edb/ref 1071 "David needs to decide what bag of flour to buy from three different types available at the grocery store:\n\n- Bag $A$ weighs $5$ kilograms, and costs $$7.50\\,.$\n\n- Bag $B$ weighs $2$ kilograms, and costs $$3.20\\,.$\n\n- Bag $C$ weighs $1$ kilogram, and costs $$1.70\\,.$\n\nWhich bag of flour is the best deal?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495092 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "60c429d6-78bf-52f4-a402-0b1e67fa2508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495092 #edb/ref 1074 "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three bags. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of kilograms.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of kilograms in bag $A$, we get\n\n$$\n7.50÷5=1.50\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $A$ is $$1.50$ per kilogram.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of kilograms in bag $B$, we get\n\n$$\n3.20÷2=1.60\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $B$ is $$1.60$ per kilogram.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of kilograms in bag $C$, we get\n\n$$\n1.70÷1=1.70\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $C$ is $$1.70$ per kilogram.\n\nFrom the above, we see that bag $A$ has the lowest unit price. Therefore, bag $A$ is the best deal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495093 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495093 #edb/ref 1069 "q-91908" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495093 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495093 #edb/ref 1071 "Lucy wants to make a cake and is going to buy some fruit to decorate. How much would it cost her to buy $\\frac{1}{8}$ of a pound of strawberries?\n\n| Item | Price |\n| --- | --- |\n| Peach | $$1.75/\\text{lb}$ |\n| Strawberries | $$2.00/\\text{lb}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495093 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6365884e-1b89-5166-9bc6-57eac1293079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495093 #edb/ref 1074 "The unit price of strawberries is $$2.00/\\text{lb}.$ To find the cost of  $\\frac{1}{8}$ of a pound of strawberries, we multiply: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\times \\frac{1}{8} & =\\frac{2}{1}\\times \\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\times 1}{1\\times 8} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =2÷8 \\\\  & =0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it would cost Lucy $$0.25$ to buy $\\frac{1}{8}$ of a pound of strawberries." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495096 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494957 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495096 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68942" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495096 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495096 #edb/ref 1071 "Bart and two of his friends pooled money together to buy a bottle of blueberry juice whose unit price is $$12.50$ per gallon. How much juice should Bart get if he only puts in $1$ dollar?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495096 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "689d0fea-adfc-579c-9b61-c303964b2aec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495096 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the unit rate of $$12.50$ per gallon (dollars per gallon). To find the amount of blueberry juice that Bart could get for $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in gallon per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n12.50\\,\\text{dollars per gallon}\\,\\to \\,1÷12.5=0.08\\,\\text{gallons per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $0.08$ gallons per dollar. Therefore, Bart should get only $0.08$ gallons of the blueberry juice." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495102 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495102 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495102 #edb/ref 1071 "Mia charges $$0.20$ per minute for babysitting. For how long did she babysit if she earns $1$ dollar?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e6dd181-7d62-533c-99d8-31ea5383ac30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495102 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the unit rate of $$0.2$ per minute (dollars per minute). To find how many minutes she babysits for  $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in minutes per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal: \n\n$$\n0.2\\,\\text{dollars per minute}\\,\\to \\,1÷0.2=5\\,\\text{minutes per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $5$ minutes per dollar. Therefore, Mia babysits $5$ minutes for $1$ dollar." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495111 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495111 #edb/ref 1069 "q-91919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495111 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495111 #edb/ref 1071 "At the vegetable market, the unit price for bananas is $$2.50$ per kilogram. How many kilograms of bananas can Mary buy with $$1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495111 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8ca824bb-dbd7-559a-8d0c-91866baabae8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495111 #edb/ref 1074 "We are given the unit rate of $$2.50$ per kilogram (dollars per kilogram). To find the number of kilograms of bananas that Mary can buy for $$1,$ we need to find the unit rate in kilograms per dollar. So, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n2.50\\,\\text{dollars per kilogram}\\,\\to \\,1÷2.50=0.4\\,\\text{kilograms per dollar}\n$$\n\nSo, the unit rate is $0.4$ kilograms per dollar. Therefore, Mary can buy $0.4$ kilograms of bananas." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495122 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495122 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495122 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495122 #edb/ref 1071 "At Sandy's shop, there are three different boxes of potato chips:\n\n- Box $A$ contains $15$ ounces, and costs $$3.00\\,.$\n\n- Box $B$ contains $18$ ounces,  and costs $$3.24\\,.$\n\n- Box $C$ contains $24$ ounces, and costs $$4.32\\,.$\n\nWhich box of potato chips is the best deal?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495122 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a79d949c-d327-5106-907b-7c4645b173b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495122 #edb/ref 1074 "The best deal is the box with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three boxes. To compute the unit price, we divide the price by the total number of ounces.   \n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n3.00÷15=0.20\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $A$ is $$0.20.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $B,$ we get\n\n$$\n3.24÷18=0.18\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $B$ is $$0.18.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n4.32÷24=0.18\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $C$ is $$0.18$ too.\n\nFrom the above, we see that boxes $B$ and  $C$ have the lowest unit price of $$0.18/\\text{oz}.$ Therefore, boxes $B$ and $C$ offer the best deal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495129 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495129 #edb/ref 1071 "At the store, there are three different boxes of corn flakes cereal.\n\n- Box $A$ contains $18$ ounces of cereal, and costs $\\[math]  3.60 \\, .$\n\n- Box $B$ contains $15$ ounces of cereal, and costs $\\[/math]  3.30 \\, .$\n\n- Box $C$ contains $20$ ounces of cereal, and costs $\\[math]  3.80 \\, .$\n\nWhich box of cereal is the best deal?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495129 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b63d3979-8319-52e5-833c-e4230a367cb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495129 #edb/ref 1074 "The best deal is the box with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three boxes. To compute the unit price, we divide the price by the total number of ounces.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n3.60 \\div 18 = 0.20 \\, .\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $A$ is $\\[math]  0.20.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $B,$ we get\n\n$$\n3.30 \\div 15 = 0.22 \\, .\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $B$ is $\\[/math]  0.22.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in box $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n3.80 \\div 20 = 0.19 \\, .\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of box $C$ is $\\[math]  0.19.$\n\nFrom the above, we see that box $C$ has the lowest unit price. Therefore, box $C$ is the best deal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495135 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495135 #edb/ref 1069 "q-68895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495135 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495135 #edb/ref 1071 "At Chan's SuperMarket, there are three different bottles of spicy peach BBQ sauce:\n\n- The $17$-ounce $A$-brand bottle costs $$14.96\\,.$\n\n- The $19$-ounce $B$-brand bottle costs $$15.96\\,.$\n\n- The $18$-ounce $C$-brand bottle costs $$15.66\\,.$\n\nWhich brand of spicy peach BBQ sauce is the best deal?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495135 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb84f58e-e39d-5a83-ae84-6b21925b6b81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495135 #edb/ref 1074 "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three brands. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of ounces.  \n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in the $A$-brand bottle,  we get\n\n$$\n14.96÷17=0.88\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of the $A$-brand bottle is $$0.88.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in the $B$-brand bottle,  we get\n\n$$\n15.96÷19=0.84\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of the $B$-brand bottle is $$0.84.$\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in the $C$-brand bottle,  we get\n\n$$\n15.66÷18=0.87\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of the $C$-brand bottle is $$0.87.$\n\nFrom the above, we see that brand $B$ has the lowest unit price. Therefore, brand $B$ is the best deal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495136 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495136 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495136 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495136 #edb/ref 1071 "Mike wants to buy some pears. The price of pears in a nearby store is $$0.95/lb.$ How much would Mike pay for $5$ pounds of pears?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495136 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bdeb06fe-5bf9-53f7-a73a-0e78ccea8201" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495136 #edb/ref 1074 "The unit price of pears is $$0.95/lb.$ To find the cost of $5$ pounds of pears, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.95\\times 5 & =4.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike would pay $$4.75$ for $5$ pounds of pears." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495143 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495143 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495143 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495143 #edb/ref 1071 "Lisa wants to buy some bulk candy. The price of candy in a nearby store is $$2.15/lb.$ How much would Lisa pay for $3$ pounds of candy?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495143 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca144f12-4645-5673-bdbf-eb03fabe7dfa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495143 #edb/ref 1074 "The unit price of candy is $$2.15/lb.$ To find the cost of $3$ pounds of candy, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.15\\times 3 & =6.45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Lisa would pay $$6.45$ for $3$ pounds of candy." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495147 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495147 #edb/ref 1069 "q-355006" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495147 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495147 #edb/ref 1071 "Sarah wonders what bag of coffee to buy from three different types available at the cafe:\n\n- Bag $A$ weighs $12$ ounces, and costs $$9.60\\,.$\n\n- Bag $B$ weighs $16$ ounces, and costs $$12.00\\,.$\n\n- Bag $C$ weighs $8$ ounces, and costs $$6.80\\,.$\n\nWhich bag of coffee is the best deal?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495147 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d34abd56-cdca-5fad-b44a-9d3b63e57048" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495147 #edb/ref 1074 "The best deal is the one with the lowest unit price. So, we need to compute the unit prices for all three bags. To compute the unit price, we divide the cost by the total number of ounces.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bag $A$, we get\n\n$$\n9.60÷12=0.80\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $A$ is $$0.80$ per ounce.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bag $B$, we get\n\n$$\n12.00÷16=0.75\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $B$ is $$0.75$ per ounce.\n\n- Dividing the total cost by the number of ounces in bag $C$, we get\n\n$$\n6.80÷8=0.85\\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price of bag $C$ is $$0.85$ per ounce.\n\nFrom the above, we see that bag $B$ has the lowest unit price. Therefore, bag $B$ is the best deal." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495154 #edb/ref 1069 "e-4539" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495154 #edb/ref 1071 "Micah is going to the store to buy some sunflower seeds. How much would it cost to buy $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a pound of sunflower seeds?\n\n| Item | Price |\n| --- | --- |\n| Pumpkin Seeds | $\\[math] 8 / \\text{lb}$ |\n| Sunflower Seeds | $\\[/math] 5 / \\text{lb}$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df05a979-ea59-5937-80a7-89e9e0014ca8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495154 #edb/ref 1074 "The price of sunflower seeds is $\\[math] 5 / \\text{lb}.$ To find the cost of  $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a pound of sunflower seeds, we multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\times \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{5}{1}\\times \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\times 1}{1\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =5÷4 \\\\  & =1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it would cost $\\[/math] 1.25$ to buy $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ of a pound of sunflower seeds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495185 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495185 #edb/ref 1061 616955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495185 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1de7948d-68f8-5999-953c-8acb8be31016" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495185 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495190 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495190 #edb/ref 1061 616954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495190 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "235b590b-52bc-52e8-a4ef-db453fdea765" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495190 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495281 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495197 #edb/ref 1061 616959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495197 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "30f25678-f130-575f-b357-4ffe8b59f407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495197 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495218 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495218 #edb/ref 1061 616950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495218 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f189f23-779a-5ab6-9245-89a7a070f017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495218 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495238 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495238 #edb/ref 1061 616958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495238 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aebf2791-ae4f-5ea2-8312-994b316164b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495238 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495245 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495245 #edb/ref 1061 616951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495245 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7fc4646-02d5-59f6-896d-aa9560955608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495245 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1031 "Working With Unit Prices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1033 6818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "04654553-f8da-5ec9-a27d-ee5bae447da5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495278 #edb/ref 1064 "For every unit price in \"dollars per item\", there is also a **reciprocal unit price** in \"items per dollar\".\n\nFor example, suppose that a store sells fabric for $\\[math]2$ per yard. Taking the reciprocal, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\textrm{dollars per yard} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2}  \\,\\textrm{yards per dollar} .\n$$\n\nSo, if we have $\\[/math]1,$ then we can buy $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ of a yard of fabric." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495278 #edb/ref 1065 3106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495278 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "18f05709-44a6-59bc-962c-dbf491ad7501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495278 #edb/ref 1067 "Reciprocal Unit Prices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495280 #edb/ref 1064 "When we compare prices, it is often helpful to know the *unit price*. Knowing it helps us:\n\n- compare different products,\n\n- determine the total cost for a given amount,\n\n- decide which option is the better deal.\n\nFor example, suppose Lena wants to buy some apples. The price of apples at a nearby market is $\\[math]1.20/\\mathrm{lb}.$ How much would Lena pay for $4$ pounds of apples?\n\nThe unit price is $\\[/math]1.20$ per pound.  \n\nSince Lena is buying $4$ pounds, we multiply the unit price by the number of pounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.20\\times 4 & =4.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Lena would pay $\\[math]4.80$ for $4$ pounds of apples.\n\nWhenever we know the unit price and the quantity, we can find the total cost by multiplying:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total Cost} = \\text{Unit Price} \\times \\text{Quantity}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495280 #edb/ref 1065 15086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495280 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "55a58a08-8164-5352-9b75-43c62ba6ecc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495280 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495281 #edb/ref 1064 "Unit prices can be used to quantify how good of a deal a particular purchase is.\n\nFor example, suppose that a store sells two packs of shirts:\n\n- $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12$\n\n- $5$ shirts for $\\[/math]15$\n\nTo determine which pack is the better deal, we can compare their unit prices.\n\n- For the pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12,$ dividing the total cost by the total number of shirts gives\n\n$$\n12 \\div 3 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price is $\\[/math]4.$\n\n- For the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15,$ dividing the total cost by the total number of shirts gives\n\n$$\n15 \\div 5 = 3.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price is $\\[/math]3.$\n\nNow we interpret the unit prices in the context of the problem.\n\n- In the pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12,$ each shirt costs $\\[/math]4.$\n\n- In the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15,$ each shirt costs $\\[/math]3.$\n\nBased on the above, we conclude that the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15$ is the better deal because it has a lower unit price (meaning that each shirt costs less)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495281 #edb/ref 1065 15097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495281 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "be64d01c-4eb7-5332-b26c-97583b224a56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495281 #edb/ref 1067 "Determining the Best Deal by Computing Unit Prices" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
